# OpenModal.ai Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is an AGI Progress Tracker Platform built with Next.js 16, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS 4, and PostgreSQL (via Neon). The platform enables community-driven capability tracking with Web3 integration, real-time filtering, predictions, and discussions around AI development timelines.

## Tech Stack
- **Frontend**: Next.js 16 (App Router), React 19, TypeScript 5, Tailwind CSS 4
- **UI Components**: shadcn/ui (Radix UI), Lucide icons, Recharts
- **Backend**: Next.js API routes, Server Actions
- **Database**: PostgreSQL (Neon serverless) with Drizzle ORM
- **Authentication**: Better Auth with SIWE (Sign In With Ethereum), ENS lookup, Google OAuth
- **Web3**: Wagmi, Viem, RainbowKit
- **Email**: Resend with React Email templates
- **Analytics**: PostHog
- **Code Quality**: Biome (linter/formatter), TypeScript strict mode

## Code Style & Formatting

### General Conventions
- Use 2-space indentation (configured in biome.json)
- Use TypeScript for all files (.ts, .tsx)
- Use functional components with hooks (no class components)
- Use async/await over promises chains
- Prefer named exports over default exports for components
- Use destructuring for props and imports
- Use template literals for string concatenation
- Keep files under 300 lines when possible

### Naming Conventions
- **Files**: kebab-case for all files (e.g., `capability-card.tsx`, `auth-client.ts`)
- **Components**: PascalCase (e.g., `CapabilityCard`, `ActivityFeed`)
- **Functions/Variables**: camelCase (e.g., `getUserCapabilities`, `isLoading`)
- **Constants**: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for true constants (e.g., `MAX_PREDICTIONS`, `API_ROUTES`)
- **Types/Interfaces**: PascalCase with descriptive names (e.g., `CapabilityWithDetails`, `PredictionFormData`)
- **Database Tables**: snake_case (following PostgreSQL convention)
- **Enums**: snake_case for values (e.g., `capability_status`, `confidence_level`)

### TypeScript Guidelines
- Enable strict mode (already configured)
- Avoid `any` types - use `unknown` if type is truly unknown
- Prefer interfaces for object shapes, types for unions/intersections
- Use Zod schemas for runtime validation (especially for forms and API inputs)
- Export types separately from implementation
- Use type inference when possible, explicit types when clarity is needed
- Prefer `type` for complex types, `interface` for extendable object shapes

### React Best Practices
- Use Server Components by default (Next.js 16 App Router)
- Add `"use client"` directive only when necessary (hooks, browser APIs, interactivity)
- Wrap client imports with dynamic imports when used in server components
- Use Suspense boundaries with fallback UI for async components
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Use React Query for client-side data fetching and caching
- Prefer composition over prop drilling (use Context sparingly)
- Memoize expensive calculations with `useMemo`, callbacks with `useCallback`

### Next.js Patterns
- Use Server Actions for data mutations (`/src/actions/`)
- Place API routes in `/src/app/api/` following Next.js conventions
- Use route groups for layout organization (e.g., `(public)`, `(dashboard)`)
- Implement loading.tsx for loading states
- Implement error.tsx for error handling
- Use `generateMetadata()` for dynamic metadata
- Optimize images with Next.js Image component
- Use `revalidatePath()` or `revalidateTag()` after mutations

## File Structure

### Directory Organization
```
src/
├── app/                    # Next.js App Router (pages, layouts, routes)
│   ├── (public)/          # Public routes group
│   ├── api/               # API routes
│   └── layout.tsx         # Root layout
├── components/            # React components
│   ├── ui/                # shadcn/ui primitives (21+ components)
│   ├── [feature]/         # Feature-specific components (e.g., agi-dashboard/, auth/)
│   └── shared/            # Reusable utilities
├── actions/               # Server Actions (grouped by domain)
├── db/                    # Database layer
│   ├── schema/            # Drizzle schemas
│   └── index.ts           # DB connection
├── lib/                   # Utilities, helpers, configs
│   ├── auth.ts            # Better Auth setup
│   ├── auth-client.ts     # Client-side auth
│   ├── email.ts           # Email service
│   └── utils.ts           # Utility functions
├── hooks/                 # Custom React hooks
├── contexts/              # React contexts
└── emails/                # Email templates
```

### File Organization Rules
- Co-locate related files (keep components near their pages when specific to one route)
- Use `_components/` folders for page-specific components
- Keep shared components in `/src/components/`
- Group server actions by domain (e.g., all capability-related actions in `capabilities.ts`)
- One component per file (except for small, tightly coupled sub-components)
- Place types in the same file as implementation, or in `types.ts` if shared

## Component Guidelines

### Component Structure
```typescript
// 1. Imports (grouped: React, Next.js, third-party, local)
import { type ReactNode } from "react"
import Link from "next/link"
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button"
import { cn } from "@/lib/utils"

// 2. Types/Interfaces
interface ComponentProps {
  title: string
  children?: ReactNode
  className?: string
}

// 3. Component definition
export function Component({ title, children, className }: ComponentProps) {
  // 4. Hooks (in order: state, effects, custom hooks)
  // 5. Event handlers
  // 6. Render logic
  return (
    <div className={cn("base-classes", className)}>
      {/* ... */}
    </div>
  )
}
```

### Server Components (Default)
- Fetch data directly in the component
- Use async/await syntax
- No need for useState, useEffect for data fetching
- Can directly access database, environment variables, server-side APIs

### Client Components
- Add `"use client"` at the top of the file
- Use for: interactivity, browser APIs, React hooks, event handlers
- Keep client components small and focused
- Prefer Server Components for data fetching, pass data as props to Client Components

### shadcn/ui Components
- Install via: `npx shadcn@latest add [component]`
- Located in `/src/components/ui/`
- Customize as needed (they're copied to your project)
- Use `cn()` utility for conditional className merging
- Follow the "new-york" style variant

## State Management

### Server State (Preferred)
- Use Server Components and Server Actions for server state
- Use React Query (TanStack Query) for client-side server state caching
- Implement optimistic updates with `useMutation`
- Use `useQuery` for GET operations, `useMutation` for mutations

### Client State
- Use `useState` for local component state
- Use `useReducer` for complex state logic
- Use Context only when necessary (avoid prop drilling for deeply nested trees)
- Keep state as local as possible

### Form State
- Use TanStack React Form for complex forms
- Use Server Actions for form submissions
- Implement Zod validation schemas
- Show loading states during submission
- Display error messages clearly

## Database & ORM

### Drizzle ORM Patterns
- Define schemas in `/src/db/schema/`
- Use snake_case for table and column names
- Group related tables in the same schema file
- Export types from schema: `export type Capability = typeof capability.$inferSelect`
- Use transactions for multi-table operations
- Prefer prepared statements for repeated queries

### Migrations
- Generate migrations: `bun run db:generate`
- Run migrations: `bun run db:migrate`
- Review generated SQL before applying
- Never edit migrations manually after they're applied

### Querying
- Use Drizzle's query builder (type-safe)
- Use `.with()` for eager loading related data
- Implement pagination with `limit()` and `offset()`
- Use `.$dynamic()` for conditional query building
- Return only needed columns with `.select()`

## Authentication

### Better Auth Setup
- Server-side auth: `@/lib/auth`
- Client-side auth: `@/lib/auth-client`
- Session provider wraps the app in `/src/app/providers.tsx`
- Use `useSession()` hook in client components
- Check auth in Server Components by importing from `@/lib/auth`
- SIWE integration for wallet authentication
- ENS lookup for wallet addresses

### Protected Routes
- Check session in page component
- Redirect to `/login` if unauthorized
- Use middleware for route protection when applicable

## API & Server Actions

### Server Actions
- Place in `/src/actions/` directory
- Add `"use server"` directive at the top
- Export async functions
- Use Zod for input validation
- Return serializable data (no class instances, functions, etc.)
- Handle errors with try-catch, return error states
- Call `revalidatePath()` or `revalidateTag()` after mutations
- Keep actions focused and single-purpose

### API Routes
- Use for: webhooks, third-party integrations, non-Next.js clients
- Prefer Server Actions for app mutations
- Return `Response` or use `NextResponse`
- Implement proper error handling (status codes)
- Validate inputs with Zod schemas

## Styling

### Tailwind CSS
- Use Tailwind classes for all styling
- Use `cn()` utility from `/src/lib/utils.ts` for conditional classes
- Follow mobile-first responsive design (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`, `2xl:`)
- Use Tailwind's spacing scale (4px increments)
- Prefer Tailwind utilities over custom CSS when possible
- Use CSS variables for theme colors (configured in `app.css`)

### Theme Support
- Use `next-themes` for dark mode
- Define colors in CSS variables
- Use semantic color names (e.g., `bg-background`, `text-foreground`)
- Test both light and dark modes

### Animations
- Use `tw-animate-css` for animations
- Keep animations subtle and purposeful
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`

## Error Handling

### Client-Side
- Use error boundaries for component errors
- Use toast notifications (Sonner) for user-facing errors
- Log errors to console in development, analytics in production
- Provide helpful error messages (avoid technical jargon)

### Server-Side
- Wrap async operations in try-catch
- Return error states from Server Actions: `{ error: string }`
- Log errors with context
- Never expose sensitive error details to client

## Performance

### Optimization Strategies
- Use Next.js Image component for all images
- Implement lazy loading with `Suspense` and `lazy()`
- Use `loading.tsx` for route loading states
- Minimize client-side JavaScript (prefer Server Components)
- Code split with dynamic imports
- Optimize bundle size (tree shaking, minimize dependencies)
- Use React Query's caching to avoid redundant requests

### Database Performance
- Use database indexes on frequently queried columns
- Implement pagination for large datasets
- Use `.select()` to limit returned columns
- Consider using database views for complex queries
- Monitor query performance with Drizzle Studio

## Testing (TODO: Not yet implemented)

### Future Testing Strategy
- Use Vitest for unit/integration tests
- Use Playwright or Cypress for E2E tests
- Test user flows, not implementation details
- Mock external dependencies (database, APIs)
- Test error states and edge cases
- Aim for meaningful coverage, not 100%

## Security

### Best Practices
- Never commit secrets (use `.env.local`, check `.env.example`)
- Validate all user inputs (Zod schemas)
- Sanitize data before rendering
- Use CSRF protection (built into Server Actions)
- Implement rate limiting for public APIs
- Use HTTPS in production
- Keep dependencies updated
- Follow OWASP guidelines

### Web3 Security
- Verify wallet signatures server-side
- Never trust client-side wallet data
- Use ENS lookup for display names only (not for auth)
- Handle wallet disconnection gracefully

## Environment Variables

### Naming Convention
- Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Prefix public vars with `NEXT_PUBLIC_`
- Document all required vars in `.env.example`
- Validate env vars at runtime (consider using Zod)

### Required Variables
```
DATABASE_URL              # Neon PostgreSQL connection string
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET        # Secret for session encryption
BETTER_AUTH_URL          # Base URL for callbacks
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID         # Google OAuth
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET     # Google OAuth
RESEND_API_KEY          # Email service
POSTHOG_KEY             # Analytics (optional)
POSTHOG_HOST            # Analytics (optional)
```

## Code Comments

### When to Comment
- Complex business logic
- Non-obvious workarounds
- TODO items with context
- Public APIs (use JSDoc)
- Regex patterns (explain the pattern)

### When NOT to Comment
- Obvious code (let the code speak)
- Redundant descriptions
- Commented-out code (use git history)

### JSDoc for Functions
```typescript
/**
 * Fetches a capability by its slug with related data
 * @param slug - The URL-friendly identifier for the capability
 * @param userId - Optional user ID to include user-specific data
 * @returns Capability with organizations, bottlenecks, and user predictions
 */
export async function getCapabilityBySlug(slug: string, userId?: string) {
  // ...
}
```

## Git Workflow

### Commit Messages
- Use conventional commits: `type(scope): message`
- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`
- Keep first line under 72 characters
- Use imperative mood: "add feature" not "added feature"

### Branch Naming
- Feature: `feat/description`
- Bug fix: `fix/description`
- Chore: `chore/description`

## Accessibility

### Guidelines
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Provide alt text for images
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Use ARIA labels when needed
- Maintain color contrast ratios (WCAG AA)
- Test with screen readers

## AI-Specific Guidelines

### When Generating Code
1. Always check existing patterns in the codebase first
2. Follow the established file structure
3. Use existing components and utilities before creating new ones
4. Match the existing code style (indentation, naming, formatting)
5. Add proper TypeScript types
6. Implement error handling
7. Consider mobile responsiveness
8. Add loading states for async operations
9. Update related documentation if adding new patterns

### When Refactoring
1. Preserve existing functionality
2. Update tests if they exist
3. Check for breaking changes
4. Update imports across the codebase
5. Verify type safety after changes

### When Debugging
1. Check the terminal for build errors
2. Review browser console for client-side errors
3. Check server logs for server-side errors
4. Verify environment variables are set
5. Check database connection and schema

## Domain-Specific Rules

### Capability Management
- All capability queries should include filtering and sorting options
- Use Drizzle's query builder for complex joins
- Implement optimistic updates for tracking/predictions
- Cache capability lists with React Query

### Comments & Voting
- Implement optimistic updates for votes
- Show loading states during submission
- Validate comment content length (max character limit)
- Support threaded discussions (parent/child relationships)

### Web3 Integration
- Handle wallet connection errors gracefully
- Support ENS name resolution
- Verify signatures server-side via Better Auth
- Provide fallback authentication (email/password)

